# Smart Ambient Lighting System - Day 2 (25.07.2026)

Today, we focused on hardware selection and "Zero-to-One Engineering Analysis". Instead of memorizing, we performed a Root Cause Analysis of how an engineer chooses parts based on the problem they encounter.

## 1. ESP32 Model Selection
We determined the most accurate model among hundreds of models available on the market for the brain of the project.
*   **Selected Model:** **ESP32 NodeMCU Development Board (ESP-WROOM-32)** (30 or 38 pins).
*   **Reason for Selection:** Having a USB port to connect directly to the computer and its own voltage regulator (5V to 3.3V) on board. Being breadboard compatible. Being able to perform PWM and Wi-Fi/MQTT operations without freezing, thanks to its dual-core architecture.
*   **Things to Avoid:** ESP8266 (Single core, old generation), ESP32-CAM (Camera module, insufficient pin count, and hard to code).

## 2. Component Search Logic in Engineering (Parametric Filtering)
The strategy for finding parts on the world's largest distributor sites (DigiKey, Mouser, etc.) without asking AI or others:
1.  **Load Analysis:** The voltage (12V) and current (5A) drawn by the LED are determined. The part to be selected is filtered with a safety margin (E.g.: >20V, >10A).
2.  **Trigger Analysis:** The ESP32 only produces 3.3V. Therefore, when selecting a MOSFET, those with 2.5V - 3.3V (Logic-Level) in the "Drive Voltage" section are filtered. (If a standard MOSFET with 10V is chosen, the ESP32 cannot open it).
3.  **Mounting Analysis:** The **TO-220** package, which can be easily hand-soldered and fitted with a heatsink, is selected.
4.  **Golden Rule:** The remaining results are sorted from highest to lowest by "Quantity Available". The part at the very top is the cheapest and most reliable part that is the industry standard (E.g.: IRLZ44N, FQP30N06L).

## 3. Root Cause Analysis: Why an N-Channel MOSFET?
The "Electrical Switch" selection process to control a massive muscle (LED) of 12V 5A with a weak brain (ESP32) of 3.3V:

*   **Why Not a Relay?** Relays are mechanical. We need to do on/off (PWM) 1000 times a second to dim the light. A mechanical part cannot move 1000 times a second; it will physically break or stick. A solid-state silicon part is mandatory.
*   **Why Not a BJT Transistor?** BJTs are current-controlled. To drive a giant load of 5 Amps, the ESP32's pin would have to supply too much current, and the chip would burn. The BJT itself also heats up a lot.
*   **Why a MOSFET?** It is voltage (electric field) controlled. It draws almost zero current from the ESP32. Feeling 3.3 Volts is enough for it to open the valve. Since its internal resistance (Rds-on) is very low, it doesn't heat up even at high currents.
*   **Why N-Channel Instead of P-Channel?** P-Channel cuts the positive (+) (High-Side Switching). If you put a valve on the 12V line, the ESP32 must also send 12V to open that valve, which requires extra complex voltage step-up components in between. The N-Channel cuts the negative (-) (Low-Side Switching). The negative (Ground/GND) of the system is common (0 Volts). It is very easy and effortless for the ESP32 to send just 3.3V relative to 0 Volts. That's why N-Channel MOSFETs are always used with microcontrollers.
